detail.abstract:
Why do systems preserve inefficiency after the crisis that produced it has passed? A review layer remains after the emergency fades. Redundancy remains after supply stabilizes. Approval slows after the original exposure disappears. A temporary restriction becomes normal. These patterns are often explained as inertia, bureaucracy, risk aversion, path dependence, or organizational habit. Such explanations may be partly correct. But they can miss the deeper movement: the crisis may have passed, but the crisis cost has not.
<div>
 This paper develops deep time as the theoretical apex and closing loop of the first Ordering Papers sequence, using institutional memory as a middle-level analytic bridge between preserved inefficiency and civilizational memory. WP05 does not merely add another application of the Ordering framework. It explains how the prior movements of the series persist across time, become survivability conditions, and return to civilizational memory as renewed constraint sensitivity. In this sense, WP05 completes the first theoretical cycle of the Ordering Papers Series.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Institutional memory does not replace civilizational memory. It names one observable form through which sedimented failure cost appears inside organizations, institutions, rules, routines, thresholds, buffers, classifications, and preserved inefficiencies. Deep time begins when that sedimented memory no longer depends on explicit narration and instead becomes a survivability condition that shapes what the system can continue to carry. The paper advances three linked mechanisms. Structural encoding explains how systems, responding to constraint and pressure and drawing from civilizational memory, convert the failure cost of a past crisis into firmware: the default operating assumptions through which future exposure is recognized. Structural repricing explains how encoded failure cost changes the ranking of options inside the decision stack, shifting layer weights and producing decision drop. Sedimentation mechanism explains how temporary protective responses, managed variables, redundancies, and defensive classifications harden into institutional memory across time.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 WP05 therefore extends structural repricing into the temporal dimension. Structural repricing was developed earlier in the series as the mechanism by which failure cost changes how systems rank options. The present paper asks what happens when that repricing persists, sediments, and becomes part of the system's memory structure. Existing accounts of path Ordering Papers Series, No. 05 3 dependence, organizational learning, and institutional inertia explain why structures persist, but they often leave under-specified how the cost of a past failure continues to price institutional behavior after the crisis itself has passed. This paper develops a deep-time sedimentation model of failure cost: a model of how crisis cost persists after crisis events end.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The central claim is that systems do not preserve inefficiency simply because they fail to update. They may preserve it because the old failure path remains structurally priced. What appears as unnecessary delay, redundancy, review, approval layering, or caution may function internally as a constraint boundary, early-warning logic, and action guide. Maturity, in this sense, is not the system remembering a crisis. Maturity is the crisis becoming structure - supplying constraint boundaries, early-warning logic, and action guidance.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The paper contributes to institutional theory, historical institutionalism, organizational learning, path dependence, risk governance, and systems analysis by specifying how past failure cost becomes a present ordering condition. It closes the first sequence of the Ordering Papers Series by linking compliance failure, failure-cost ranking, external-actor reclassification, decision drop, sedimentation, deep time, institutional memory, and civilizational memory into a single ordering cycle. Civilizational memory appears both as the background from which constraint sensitivity begins and as the repository into which deeply sedimented ordering cycles return. It is therefore not merely a starting premise. It is also the renewed foundation for the next cycle of structural encoding.&nbsp;
</div>

